Friction does not conserve energy, since momentum is lost to the medium in the form of heat. It is irreversible: energy is lost by the object but not regained. Friction forces must change signs under $bold(v) -> -bold(v)$, hence they must depend on velocity; in doubt, a friction force should slow the object down.

As far as we know, the fundamental laws of nature are invariant under CPT, so friction forces cannot be fundamental forces.
